  </div>

  <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;margin:16px 0 8px;color:var(--accent)">Condition Sourcing</h3>
  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Source Deadstock whenever possible.</strong> The {fmt_dollar(ds_used_gap)} margin gap between Deadstock and Used condition is the single largest value driver in the dataset. Deadstock pairs achieve a {fmt_pct(condition_stats.loc["Deadstock (Brand New)", "premium_rate"])} premium rate vs. just {fmt_pct(condition_stats.loc["Used", "premium_rate"])} for Used. VNDS inventory can be viable as a middle-ground strategy at {fmt_dollar(vnds_margin)} average margin, but requires careful cost management since the margin buffer is thin.
  </div>

  <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;margin:16px 0 8px;color:var(--accent)">Channel Strategy</h3>
  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>For maximum margin, sell on {best_ch_margin}</strong> (avg margin: {fmt_dollar(best_ch_margin_val)}). <strong>For fastest turnover, use {best_ch_velocity}</strong> (avg {fmt(best_ch_velocity_val, 1)} days in inventory). On an <strong>annualized return basis</strong> (margin &times; 365/days), <strong>{best_annualized_ch}</strong> offers the highest effective return at {fmt_dollar(ch_annualized[best_annualized_ch])}/unit/year. When capital is limited, prioritize velocity; when inventory capacity is the constraint, optimize for margin per unit.
  </div>

  <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;margin:16px 0 8px;color:var(--accent)">Timing & Seasonality</h3>
  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>List aggressively in {month_names[best_month_idx - 1]}</strong> when average margins peak at {fmt_dollar(month_seasonality.loc[best_month_idx, 'avg_margin'])}. Consider building inventory in {month_names[worst_month_idx - 1]} (avg margin: {fmt_dollar(month_seasonality.loc[worst_month_idx, 'avg_margin'])}) when prices are lower, then sell into seasonal strength. This buy-low, sell-high cadence can add {fmt_dollar(month_seasonality.loc[best_month_idx, 'avg_margin'] - month_seasonality.loc[worst_month_idx, 'avg_margin'])} per unit in margin improvement.
  </div>

  <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;margin:16px 0 8px;color:var(--accent)">Inventory Holding Discipline</h3>
  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>The overall average of {fmt(avg_days_inventory, 1)} days in inventory sets a baseline,</strong> but optimal holding time varies by channel and model. Pairs sitting beyond {fmt(avg_days_inventory + profit_margin_std, 0)} days on slow channels should be repriced or moved to faster channels. The data shows no consistent correlation between longer hold times and higher margins, suggesting that time-in-inventory is more a function of initial pricing than patient waiting for a better offer.
  </div>

  <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;margin:16px 0 8px;color:var(--accent)">Risk Factors</h3>
  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>{fmt_pct(100 - resale_premium_rate)} of all transactions sell at or below retail.</strong> This underscores that the resale market is not a guaranteed profit engine. The highest risk segments are Used-condition {worst_model} pairs, which consistently sell below retail. Diversify across models and conditions, and never source inventory assuming a premium — always model downside scenarios where the pair sells at or near retail.
  </div>
</div>

